# would run (scanner_version; a push with nothing to scan reads none): the
# options the hook passes need 8.24.0 (the report template since 8.21.3, the
# byte figure since 8.21.4, the pkcs12-file rule since 8.24.0), and this
# repository's .gitleaks.toml uses the [[allowlists]] form, which 8.24.0 and
# 8.24.3 ignore and 8.25.0 is the first to read as written (the round 9
# rulings, 2026-09-25, each release run against the hook). A version below
# the floor, or an answer that is not a dotted version (empty, cut, or a
# build from source's), refuses the push as a scan that cannot run, the line
# naming what was found, the floor and the remedies (upgrade it; point
# ROMP_GITLEAKS at another binary; ROMP_NO_GITLEAKS=1 for one push). CI's
# pinned gitleaks, 8.28.0, is above the floor.
# A second, ADDITIVE run covers the five default rules that fire only on a
# file's path (pkcs12-file, nuget-config-password, kubernetes-secret-yaml,
# hashicorp-tf-password, freemius-secret-key), which the pieces' digit names
# cannot trigger: each piece of a file whose name ends in .p12, .pfx,
# nuget.config, .yaml, .yml, .tf, .hcl or .php (in any case), a merge's
# binary path read whole among them, is copied under a name made of the
# piece's number and that suffix, lower-cased (12.yaml, 12.p12), or as
# nuget.config, each copy in a directory named by its piece, and gitleaks
# scans the copies with those rules alone (--enable-rule), its byte figure
# checked the same way against the bytes copied. Each of the five rules'
# paths matches such a name, and no copy's name is taken from the path: until
# round 10b a copy took the path's basename as git quotes it, escapes kept,
# and a selected file whose quoted basename passed 255 bytes (a byte past
# ASCII costs four, a double quote or a backslash two) ended the hook with
# bash's File name too long line and no romp line on a clean push (the round
# 9 refuters, 2026-09-25); a copy named by the decoded basename would be
# named past ASCII, and gitleaks' directory scan skipped one such copy
# (pnpm-loc<U+212A>.yaml), refusing a clean push on the figure. A file
# gitleaks' global path allowlist skips by its name is left out of this run,
# by the rule stated here: a name holding gitleaks.toml as spelled, one
# holding verification-metadata.xml or Database, any byte, refactorlog, and
# one that is pnpm-lock.yaml (those three in any case), which gitleaks 8.28.0
# and 8.30.1 skip under those names (probed; until round 10b the copy carried
# the file's name, so gitleaks skipped such a copy and the figure could be
# exact only with the file left out); a file its git mode skips by a
# directory in its path (node_modules, a vendored tree) has no directory here
# and is scanned. gitleaks evaluates every path-keyed element of the config
# against a copy's name, not the file's path, so a per-rule or targeted path
# allowlist, in either condition, or a rule's own path, that treats a copy's
# name differently from the file's path changes that rule's verdict in the
# copy without skipping it, and the figure agrees (until round 11 a per-rule
# allowlist on nuget.config, or on the number directory 1/, published a
# credential main's hook refused; the round 10 rulings, 2026-09-26). So a
# third run, the PROBE run, reads one more directory holding, for each
# selected piece, a PROBE at the copy's exact name (12/12.yaml), a short
# text the rule reports under gitleaks' default rules, and a SECOND COPY of
# the piece at the directory's root, named romp-copy-, the piece's number in
# letters (a to j for 0 to 9), a dot when the suffix holds none, and the
# file's own suffix as it spells it (romp-copy-bc.yaml,
# romp-copy-bc.NuGet.Config): a name with no digit and no directory, which
# each rule's default path matches and no global path of the default skips.
# It runs with the five rules alone and the same config, its figure checked
# against the bytes written there; its exit 2 is expected, its findings (the
# probes among them) are never shown, and its log is shown only when it
# fails. A probe its report does not hold under the probe's own rule (the
# file and the rule, never the file alone) refuses the push, the line naming
# the rule, the file's path, the copy's name and the config read, and saying
# that config dropped the rule's probe at the copy's name. The hook learns
# this by the effect alone, so the line names no entry and does not say a
# path entry did it: a regex or stopword allowlist, or a raised entropy
# floor, that matches a probe's value drops the probe as surely. The probes'
# values are chosen so that the repository's own allowlists are the only
# thing that can drop them (each clear of the default config's value
# allowlists, stopwords and entropy floors). The line's remedies, in order:
# excuse a false alarm by its value with a regex or stopword allowlist
# instead of a path; keep the rule's default path; ROMP_NO_GITLEAKS=1 for
# one push. A finding at a second copy refuses as a credential, named by
# commit and file: it catches an AND-condition path allowlist, whose drop
# depends on the content too, which no probe can see. The cost, with its
# witnesses in tests/pre-push-hook.bats' round 11b section: a push adding a
# selected file, under a config that drops the file's rule at the copy's
# name, is refused even when the file is clean (a clean prod.nuget.config
# under a per-rule allowlist on (^|/)nuget\.config$), and also where the
# file's own path gets the same verdict (an unanchored per-rule
# (?i)nuget\.config$ allowlist excuses the rule for every nuget.config, and
# every push adding one is refused), each a push main's hook passes; a
# narrower refusal is a held follow-up; and every push with a selected file
# pays one more scanner run and one awk, no process per piece. The
# residual, with its witness there: an AND-condition allowlist whose path
# matches both the copy's name and the second copy's (^(?:[0-9]+/|romp-copy-))
# and not the file's path, with value criteria matching the credential,
# publishes it; such a path keys on the hook's synthetic names and has no
# honest use, and what it publishes is a value the config excuses by value.
# A global path allowlist that matches a copy's name skips the copy and
# refuses the push on the figure; one keyed on the file's real path
# (^deploy/secret\.yaml$) matches neither name, so the copy is scanned and a
# credential there refused where main passes, the safe side; and a config
# that drops any of the five rules makes the run fail, refused as a scan
# that could not complete.
# The config every run is handed is READ first, by the shell, where a scan
# would run (gitleaks_config): the work tree's .gitleaks.toml and every file
# its [extend] chain names, or, with none, the file GITLEAKS_CONFIG names or
# GITLEAKS_CONFIG_TOML's text, the order gitleaks takes them in; gitleaks'
# default config is never read, since the additive run owns its five paths.
# Every run is then handed the reader's own copy of what it read, written to
# the scratch directory (the owner's call, ruled 2026-09-26), so the reader's
# verdict and the scanner's input are the same bytes, whatever changes a
# file after the read.
# A repository config whose rules carry a path condition (a path key on a
# [[rules]] table, the rule's own, not an allowlist's paths) refuses the
# push, one line per rule naming its id and the file: the scan reads the
# pushed lines as pieces named by number and cannot apply the condition,
# which gitleaks evaluates against a piece's name, where it never fires (a
# custom rule on .env, a path-only rule on .jks, generic-api-key given a .py
# path and hashicorp-tf-password widened to .tfvars each published a
# credential until round 11; the round 10 checkers, 2026-09-26). Exact
# support for path-conditioned rules is a held follow-up. Two path values
# are exempt, each on a path key standing alone: on one of the five
# path-scoped rules, the text the hook's own table gives that rule (the path
# gitleaks' default config gives it, which the additive run's copies meet),
# byte for byte in a literal string, so a config copied from gitleaks'
# default passes; and on any rule, an empty string, which gitleaks reads as
# no condition. The exemption is keyed on the hook's table, not on the
# running gitleaks' default, which the hook cannot read. The reader fails
# closed: a construct it does not parse (the rules written as a key, an
# inline table or array of them; a string it cannot delimit; a table header
# it does not recognize; a quoted key holding an escape or a byte outside
# printable ASCII, which gitleaks decodes and case-folds; an [extend] path
# that is not a one-line string; a file it cannot open) refuses the push
# naming it and its line, and each file's bytes as the shell read them are
# compared with its byte count, so a read cut short refuses too. These
# refusals come before either scanner run, and each line says the scan
# follows once the config is fixed. A relative [extend] path is resolved
# against the directory git runs the hook in (the work tree's root), as
# gitleaks resolved it while the hook ran gitleaks there, before round 9, at
# each of the two levels gitleaks follows, and the copy names it absolutely
# (until round 11 a relative extend refused every push with content on
# gitleaks' own FTL line; the coordinator's decision 5, 2026-09-26); a path
# at the third level, which gitleaks ignores, is refused, a visible refusal
# of a rare shape, since a later gitleaks that followed it would read rules
# the reader never saw; and a relative GITLEAKS_CONFIG is made absolute the
# same way.
# What the pieces cost, as three facts: gitleaks' path exemptions (lockfiles,
# node_modules and vendored trees, images, fonts, documents, go.sum and the
# rest of its global allowlist, and its per-rule path allowlists) do not reach
# the main run, so a credential-shaped string in such a file is refused, and a
# false alarm there is excused in .gitleaks.toml by exact value; a match
# with more than 16,384 of its bytes before a piece boundary, in a file whose
# added lines pass 98,304 bytes or in a line longer than 81,916 bytes (cut
# into windows), is whole in neither piece (the replay and the windows'
# overlap repeat exactly the last 16,384 bytes, so what decides is how many
# of the match's bytes lie before the boundary, not its length: one with at
# most 16,384 before it is whole in the next piece; the round 9 rulings,
# 2026-09-25, and the round 10b witnesses in tests/pre-push-hook.bats, which
# place a match at the bound and one byte past it); and a piece whose bytes
# carry a binary type's signature at its fixed offset (DICOM at 128, tar at
# 257, ISO 9660 at 32,769) is skipped by gitleaks and refused by the byte
# figure, loudly, never published. The byte figure is a log line gitleaks does
# not document (scanned ~N bytes); it equalled the bytes read on every input
# tried, in 8.28.0 and 8.30.1, from 0 bytes to 178 MB, and a gitleaks that
# stops writing it refuses every push with content, visibly, where
# ROMP_NO_GITLEAKS=1 skips one push. Until round 9 gitleaks ran git log -p
# itself, and the hook judged its log by an ERR line and a commit count, whole
# at the granularity of commits: a stream cut inside a commit, by a git on
# PATH or a real git killed partway, published a credential through a real
# push under gitleaks 8.30.1 and 8.28.0 (the round 8 refuters, 2026-09-24).
# The two halves differ in kind: the credential feed is plumbing given each
# option explicitly, and gitleaks reads only the bytes the hook wrote, while
# the identifier half reads a log and diff stream and is config-dependent by
# construction. What can hide a blob from its two content reads is closed by a
# derived property rather than key by key (refuse_hidden_paths: git's own
# verdict against the blob's bytes, which covers the diff attribute and
# core.bigFileThreshold today and a key found tomorrow the same day, in a
# one-parent commit's diff and in a merge's combined patch alike); the
# identifier half's other reads are hardened key by key as keys are found
# (colour on its grep, the signature report on its log, each by the option
# that outranks the key). Reading the identifier half's input from the objects
# is the long fix, not taken in this fold.
#
# Both scans read the objects at the pushed shas, on the premise that what they
# read is what the push transfers. A replace ref (git replace) breaks it: every
# read follows the substitute while the push transfers the original, and a real
# push published a banned string and a credential at the pushed sha with both
# scans reading clean (2026-09-21). So every git the hook runs (gitleaks runs
# none since round 9) runs with replacement OFF (GIT_NO_REPLACE_OBJECTS), and a clone that
# carries any replace ref at all is refused before either scan
# (refuse_replace_refs): a scan under replacement cannot vouch for the bytes
# pushed. Only where a scan would otherwise run, though: the refusal exists
# because a replace ref makes the scanned bytes diverge from the transferred
# bytes, so a CLEAN REPORT would be false; where no scan runs (no denylist
# file, and gitleaks skipped or absent) there is no report and nothing false to
# prevent, and a clone that never asked for either scan is unaffected, as
# CLAUDE.md promises. The two arming tests (identifier_scan_armed,
# credential_scan_armed) are the ones the scans' own early returns read, so the
# gate and the scans agree on whether a scan is ARMED. The gate is per clone,
# not per ref: a push a scan would read nothing of (a deletion of a remote ref
# alone, which both scans skip before reading a byte; a denylist holding only
# comments, which arms a scan that greps for nothing) is refused under a
# replace ref too, the safe side, and moving the gate into the two per-ref
# loops would refuse twice.
#
# Why pushed commits rather than a working-tree scan: the hook is symlinked into
# the SHARED git hooks dir (by install.sh), so it fires from every worktree —
# but a working-tree scan is only as good as the tree you happen to push from,
# and an untracked scanner script exists in only one worktree. Reading the
# denylist from $HOME and reading the pushed shas arms every worktree equally.
#
# Env: ROMP_PRIVATE_STRINGS (denylist path), ROMP_GITLEAKS (scanner binary),
# ROMP_NO_GITLEAKS=1 (skip the credential scan and its notice); gitleaks' own
# GITLEAKS_CONFIG and GITLEAKS_CONFIG_TOML are read, as gitleaks reads them,
# when the work tree holds no .gitleaks.toml.
# Bypass a false positive from either scan with `git push --no-verify`.
set -euo pipefail
# Replacement off for every git below (the header's premise): the scans read
# the objects the push transfers, never a substitute.
export GIT_NO_REPLACE_OBJECTS=1
# git feeds the ref list on stdin and both scans need it, so read it once,
# with the shell's own read builtin, and FIRST: no tool runs before the list
# is read, neither on PATH between git and the two loops (a cat that exited 0
# printing nothing left refs empty, both loops ran zero times, and every ref
# of the push was published unscanned with nothing printed; the round 6
# refuters, 2026-09-22) nor ahead of the read as a child that inherits the
# hook's stdin (a configuration read, of log.showRoot, ran here until round 8
# and was retired in round 9, and a git that drained stdin on it, exit 0 and
# nothing printed, emptied the list the same way through a real push; the
# round 7 refuters, 2026-09-23). The
# `|| [ -n "$line" ]` keeps a last line git wrote without a newline. line is
# UNSET on purpose (the unset below guards against a name the environment
# exported): read returns 1 at the end of the input and on a read error
# alike, and on an error it assigns nothing, so the test on the unset name
# ends the hook under set -u with a status, which refuses the push, where a
# name set beforehand would read the error as the end of the list. An empty
# list is git's own answer for a push with nothing to update, and passes as
# it did.
# outside judged_read: stdin, not a read of an object.
refs=""
unset line
while IFS= read -r line || [ -n "$line" ]; do
    refs="$refs$line"$'\n'
done
refs=${refs%$'\n'}

zero=0000000000000000000000000000000000000000
blocked_ids=0
blocked_addresses=0     # a subset of blocked_ids, with a remedy of its own
blocked_messages=0      # likewise a subset of blocked_ids, with a remedy of its own
blocked_tags=0          # an annotated tag's own tagger or message: one of the two above, with a remedy of its own
blocked_tag_addresses=0 # a subset of blocked_tags: a tag's own tagger address, whose remedy waits on the chosen-addresses read
blocked_tag_messages=0  # a subset of blocked_tags: a tag's own message, whose remedy (re-create the tag) needs no chosen address
blocked_creds=0
failed_scan=0           # a read or scan that could not complete: refused as unscanned (unscanned, the gitleaks arm)
unscanned_n=0           # every refusal unscanned made, counted: list_commits compares it before and after to tell its own refusal, which failed_scan (already 1, perhaps) cannot
failed_creds=0          # a subset of failed_scan: gitleaks itself could not scan, with advice of its own
hidden_paths=0          # a subset of failed_scan: git calls a text file binary and the identifier scan skipped it, with a remedy of its own
hidden_unnamed=0        # a subset of hidden_paths: no diff attribute accounts for the verdict, so the advice names the configuration key too
hidden_attr=0           # a subset of hidden_paths: a refused line named the path's diff attribute as the cause, or could not read it, so the advice names the attribute's remedy
hidden_prior=0          # a subset of hidden_paths: a changed file whose previous version (in the commit's parent, at the path a rename came from, or in any parent of a merge) is binary by its bytes, with the fetch remedy and the attribute line for the previous version's path
scratch=""              # a directory for the added-lines capture (scan_identifiers), the NUL-delimited listings the binary-verdict check reads (refuse_hidden_paths) and the credential feed (scan_credentials); made on first use (make_scratch), removed by the EXIT trap below
read_rc=0               # judged_read's two answers for the read just made: its status, and its output when no file took it
read_out=""
# The scratch directory is removed from here, on EXIT, by its exact minted
# path and never a glob (the header's scratch paragraph): a clean pass, a
# refused push and a bash line ending the hook under set -e (a scratch
# directory that cannot be written into) leave nothing under TMPDIR alike,
# where the removal at the end of the identifier scan ran on the roads that
# reached it and a hook ended by bash left the directory behind (the round 7
# refuter, 2026-09-23).
trap '[ -z "$scratch" ] || rm -rf -- "$scratch"' EXIT

# The commits THIS push publishes for one ref: what no remote already has.
# "Already has" means reachable from the remote ref's current value or from ANY
# remote-tracking ref this clone holds (refs/remotes/*, as last fetched) — every
# remote's, not only the pushed-to remote's or the ref being pushed. The
# difference from the ref's plain update range is a branch that merged main: its
# range holds main's commits, every one of them published already through main.
# Scanning those refuses the push over a string that main itself published, and a
# string already on a remote is fixed FORWARD, on main (redact, push main) — not
# by blocking every branch that merged main before the redaction. Every remote
# counts because that remedy exists only for a commit no remote has yet: in a
# clone with a fork and the project it forked from, a clean branch cut from the
# project's main is pushed to the fork as a new ref, and an exclusion scoped to
# the fork left the project's own commits in the range — one named as adding a
# string the project had since redacted, which no rewrite of the branch could
# fix (the #968 review). With no remote-tracking refs at all (never fetched) the
# exclusion matches nothing and the range falls back to everything the remote
# ref lacks — for a new ref, the whole history.
rev_range() {   # <local_sha> <remote_sha> → a rev-list argument list
    local args="$1 --not --remotes"
    [ "$2" = "$zero" ] || args="$args $2"
    printf '%s' "$args"
}

# The lines a commit adds, one per line as "<path>\t<text>". A commit with one
# parent: its diff against that parent (a root commit, against the empty tree).
# A merge: the combined diff against ALL its parents (-c), keeping only the lines
# in NONE of them — a conflict resolution, a line typed into the merge — which is
# what the merge itself adds. A diff against the first parent alone named a merge
# of main, taken while main carried a string, as adding it: the content came in
# through the second parent, which the remote already had, and the branch's tip
# was clean (the #968 review). In a combined diff every content line carries one
# column per parent, and a line in no parent has a `+` in each; -c also omits a
# file that matches any parent outright, which holds no such line. -M keeps a
# rename from re-adding every line of the file. A new symlink shows up here too:
# a link's target is its blob content, so it is an added line. The path comes
# from the `+++ b/<path>` header, read only between a `diff --git` or
# `diff --combined` line and the first hunk: inside a hunk, a line that begins
# with `+++ ` is added content (`++ b/x` as written) and belongs to the current
# file. Two reads, made apart by the caller so a refusal can name the one that
# failed: the parent count (read in scan_identifiers, in bash: rev-list
# --parents -n 1 prints the commit and then each parent, so its answer is
# judged against the commit named first once the read exited 0, and a failure
# or a short answer leaves n empty, which awk reads as 1, so a merge's
# combined diff is read in one column: over-scan, but a read refused all the
# same) and the diff (added_lines, a pipeline passed to judged_read whole,
# whose status is the diff-tree's or the awk's through pipefail). Each stage
# has a sibling fact of its own (the header's second class). The git stage:
# --always (in place of --no-commit-id) makes diff-tree print the commit's
# own sha as its FIRST line whatever the diff holds, a root commit, an empty
# commit and a merge with no line of its own included, so a git that exits 0
# printing nothing is told apart from a commit adding nothing: the awk
# records its count only after that marker line, and records the word
# unmarked otherwise, which the caller refuses naming the git read (a
# diff-tree exiting 0 and printing nothing was read as a commit adding no
# line, and a banned line in a one-parent middle commit published through a
# real push; the round 7 refuters, 2026-09-23); the marker bounds the
# answer's start alone (the header). Its END: the commit is given on
# diff-tree's stdin (--stdin) twice, the second time as its own parent
# (<rev> <rev>), an empty diff for which --always prints the sha a second
# time, AFTER the diff: the tail marker. The awk records its count only when
# the answer ends with that line, and the word untailed otherwise (no tail,
# or a line after it), which the caller refuses naming the git read (a
# diff-tree cut to its marker line, or cut before the added line, with exit
# 0 published a middle commit's banned line through a real push; the r8b2
# audit, 2026-09-23). No line of a diff is a commit's bare name: a content
# line carries its column prefix and a header line its keyword, so the two
# markers are the only such lines. The awk stage: the awk RECORDS the
# count of lines it printed, at its end, in the file the caller names
# (through the environment, never a -v operand: awk escape-processes a -v
# value, and a path may carry a backslash pair; the sha is hex and travels
# by -v): the caller compares it with the capture's line count and refuses a
# difference, or no count at all, where an awk exiting 0 and printing
# nothing was read as a commit adding no line and published its banned line
# through a real push (the round 6 refuters, 2026-09-22).
added_lines() {   # <rev> <parent count> <count file: the count of lines printed, written at the end; the word unmarked when the diff never printed the commit's sha, untailed when it never printed it again after the diff>
    git diff-tree -p -r -M -c --root --always --no-color --stdin <<< "$1"$'\n'"$1 $1" | ROMP_COUNT_FILE=$3 awk -v n="$2" -v sha="$1" '
        BEGIN { if (n < 1) n = 1; for (i = 0; i < n; i++) plus = plus "+"; count_file = ENVIRON["ROMP_COUNT_FILE"] }
        NR == 1 && $0 == sha             { marker = 1; next }
        tail                             { after = 1; next }
        marker && $0 == sha              { tail = 1; next }
        /^diff --(git|combined) /        { hdr = 1; next }
        hdr && /^\+\+\+ b\//             { f = substr($0, 7); next }
        hdr && /^\+\+\+ /                { f = $0; next }
        /^@@/                            { hdr = 0; next }
        !hdr && substr($0, 1, n) == plus { print f "\t" substr($0, n + 1); printed++ }
        END { if (!marker) print "unmarked" > count_file; else if (!tail || after) print "untailed" > count_file; else print printed + 0 > count_file }'
}

# The addresses this clone publishes under BY CHOICE: user.email from any config
# scope (an includeIf's included, as git resolves it for this repository) and the
# environment's overrides, one per line, in chosen (the caller's). An unset
# user.email contributes nothing (git config exits 1 with no value: the object's
# own answer, own=), so on a clone that never set one every stamped address is
# checked. Blank lines are harmless: a non-empty address never matches one whole.
# The config read goes through judged_read (expected 0 and 1; -q, since git's
# own line for a broken configuration file names nothing a pusher can act on
# here beyond the read the refusal names) and a failure is refused as unscanned
# naming the read, with chosen_unread holding its status so the address lines
# in scan_identifiers lead with that cause. Until round 7b the failure was
# swallowed as the strict side (no address chosen, so every stamped address
# judged), and a commit stamped under the very address the clone IS configured
# to use was refused as "an address this clone is not configured to use", a
# false cause with an inapplicable remedy (the round 6 refuters, by a real push,
# 2026-09-22). Called in the shell, never in a substitution: the refusal's
# counter and chosen_unread would be set in a subshell and lost. The answer's
# END: git ends every address it prints with a newline, which the capture keeps
# (-k), and an address counts only when that newline follows it: an answer cut
# inside its last address leaves that address unterminated, and it is dropped
# rather than chosen as the prefix it was cut to, so a cut answer chooses fewer
# addresses, the strict side (a cut of dev@testhost.example.org to
# dev@testhost.example chose the address a commit was stamped with, and its
# banned domain went ungrepped through a real push; round 8b3, 2026-09-23).
chosen_emails() {
    local got
    chosen=""; chosen_unread=0
    judged_read own="the CHOSEN ADDRESSES of this clone" 0,1 "the ADDRESSES this clone is configured to use could not be read (git config --get-all user.email exited {rc}), so whether a stamped address is one it chose is unknown" -q -k -- git config --get-all user.email || chosen_unread=$read_rc
    got=$read_out
    case "$got" in *$'\n') ;; *) got=${got%"${got##*$'\n'}"} ;; esac     # an unterminated last address is a cut one: dropped
    chosen=$got$'\n'$(printf '%s\n' "${GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL:-}" "${GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL:-}" "${EMAIL:-}")
}

# Whether an address is one this clone chose: matched whole and
# case-insensitively by the shell alone, a case statement under nocasematch
# (bash 3.1 and later; set for the comparison and put back as it was). No
# tool runs here on purpose: the match's PERMISSIVE verdict is the exit-0
# side, the one answer the helper's gates cannot judge, and a grep -q here
# that exited 0 printing nothing made every stamped address chosen, so a
# commit and a tag stamped under a banned domain published through real
# pushes with nothing printed (the round 7 refuter, 2026-09-23). A blank line
# among the chosen addresses matches no address, since both callers skip an
# empty address before asking. Called as a condition.
is_chosen() {   # <address> <chosen addresses, one per line>
    local c was=0
    if shopt -q nocasematch; then was=1; fi
    shopt -s nocasematch
    while IFS= read -r c; do
        case "$1" in "$c") [ "$was" -ne 0 ] || shopt -u nocasematch; return 0 ;; esac
    done <<< "$2"
    [ "$was" -ne 0 ] || shopt -u nocasematch
    return 1
}

# An annotated tag's header fields (`object`, `tagger`) and its message are
# read from ONE capture of the tag object and parsed by the shell in
# scan_identifiers (the header ends at the first BLANK line, one with no
# non-blank byte, and the message is the rest, a signed tag's signature
# included; it is text like any other): no
# awk stands between the object and the three checks (round 7; the two awk
# readers this replaced exited 0 printing nothing under a shim and published a
# banned tagger and a banned message through real pushes, the round 6
# refuters, 2026-09-22).

# A read that FAILED (git's status, or an error it printed; an absent field or an
# empty tree prints nothing and exits 0): the push is then only part-scanned, and
# what went unread may be exactly the string the scan exists for. Report it and
# refuse the push as unscanned, the road the credential scan takes when gitleaks
# cannot run. Found by execution (2026-09-20), with each such read counted as
# empty instead: a `cat-file -p` that failed while `cat-file -t` still said tag
# published a tag whose tagger domain was on the denylist; a git grep exiting 2,
# an ls-tree that failed after listing the link, a link blob that would not read
# and a failed type read each published a tip that inherited main's leak; nothing
# printed, every counter at zero. A per-commit diff-tree or log that failed (a
# shim; the diff-tree with the real git too, the added file's blob gone from the
# store) published a commit that added a banned line, one authored under a banned
# domain and one whose message named a banned host, git's own error line the only
# sign: piped into the grep or the loop that judged it, the read's failure was a
# no-match. Each caller says what could not be read; the tail and the counters are
# the same for all (unscanned_n counts the refusals, so a caller can tell one of
# its own: list_commits). The credential scan's feed is judged the same way, and
# gitleaks' read of it by the byte figure gitleaks logs (scan_credentials).
unscanned() {   # <what could not be read, as a clause>
    echo "romp pre-push: $1; the scan is incomplete, so the push is refused" >&2
    failed_scan=1; unscanned_n=$((unscanned_n + 1))
}
# The function below builds the CLAUSE judged_read is given for a type read
# that fails (no status in the line: the read prints its own error line ahead
# of it where it prints one).
# The TYPE read decides whether there is a tag to scan at all, on the pushed
# object and on each one the peel reaches; read as "not a tag" when it failed, it
# left the tag unpeeled and unscanned. A branch is pushed through this read too,
# so the line names the object the ref pushes, not "the tag".
type_unread() {   # <local_ref> <sha>: the clause
    printf '%s' "the TYPE of the object $1 pushes (${2:0:10}) could not be read, so whether it is an annotated tag is unknown"
}

# Every read the hook judges goes through here: the header's two classes,
# closed by construction rather than read by read. The command runs with its
# status captured in read_rc and its output in read_out (or written to the
# file -o names, or appended to the file -a names: a NUL-delimited listing, or
# the added lines, which a substitution would mangle), and a status outside the
# read's EXPECTED set (a grep: 0 for a match and 1 for none; a git: 0; a set
# stated at each call, with the reason where it is wider) refuses the push as
# unscanned with the caller's clause, {rc} in it replaced by the status. The
# first argument is the read's TAG, the second class's answer for it:
#   gate="<name>"  the caller judges an exit-0 EMPTY or SHORT answer after the
#                  call against a sibling fact in hand, named at the call site,
#                  and a disagreement refuses the push naming both answers
#   own="<name>"   an exit-0 empty answer is the object's own (a clean tree, a
#                  commit adding no line, a tag with no message) and passes;
#                  no sibling fact exists to check it against
# The header's two lists are DERIVED from these tags: tests/pre-push-hook.bats
# reads the tags out of this file and fails when either list names anything
# else, so the lists are never written by hand, and a new call site without a
# tag, or with a name the header lacks, is red there. Options before the --:
# -q sends the command's stderr to /dev/null (the reads whose git error line
# the hook does not show), -e merges it into the output (the tip's grep, which
# tells its error lines apart), -k keeps the output's trailing newlines (a
# symlink target of a newline alone is not an empty target), -o <file> and
# -a <file> write or append the output to a file instead of read_out (the
# file is opened ONCE, to a descriptor of this shell, before the command
# runs, and the command writes to that descriptor: a failed open is refused
# whatever the expected set, naming the file, since a command not run has no
# status the set could admit; a redirect on the command itself failed with
# status 1, which a set holding 1 read as a grep's no-match, and a leaking
# commit was published through a real push with the hits file made a
# directory, the round 6 refuters, 2026-09-22; one open, so no second point
# of failure sits between a check and the write); -d <function> names a
# function run in a command substitution (a subshell) after a read the set
# refuses, so it may only PRINT: its output replaces {detail} in the clause,
# for a fact known only once the read has run (the join's short file, whether
# it was written before the pipeline failed); it writes no caller variable (a
# local of its own is fine; an assignment to the caller's is lost with the
# subshell) and makes no read of its own, neither a judged_read (its refusal
# would set failed_scan in the subshell and be lost) nor one outside the
# helper. A
# pipeline is passed as a function whose status is the pipeline's (pipefail),
# so every stage's status reaches here; no command run here is a judged_read
# of its own (in a substitution its refusal would set failed_scan in a
# subshell and be lost, and in -o mode it would overwrite read_rc). Returns 0
# when the status is in the set (the caller then judges the answer) and 1 when
# the read was refused here; read_out holds whatever the command printed
# either way, so a caller that judges a partial answer too (a hit ahead of a
# failure is still named) calls with `|| :`, since under set -e a bare refused
# call would end the hook before its verdict.
judged_read() {   # <gate="name"|own="name"> <expected statuses, comma-separated> <clause, {rc} the status, {detail} the -d function's answer> [-q|-e] [-k] [-d <function>] [-o <file>|-a <file>] -- <command...>
    local expect="$2" clause="$3" errs=inherit keep=0 out="" append=0 detail="" opened=1
    shift 3
    while [ "$1" != -- ]; do
        case "$1" in
            -q) errs=quiet ;;
            -e) errs=merge ;;
            -k) keep=1 ;;
            -d) detail="$2"; shift ;;
            -o) out="$2"; shift ;;
            -a) out="$2"; append=1; shift ;;
        esac
        shift
    done
    shift
    read_rc=0; read_out=""
    if [ -n "$out" ]; then
        # The one open (the comment above): descriptor 9, closed again below.
        # bash's own line for a failed open prints ahead of the refusal.
        if [ "$append" -ne 0 ]; then { exec 9>>"$out"; } || opened=0; else { exec 9>"$out"; } || opened=0; fi
        if [ "$opened" -eq 0 ]; then
            case "$clause" in
                *"exited {rc}"*) clause=${clause//exited \{rc\}/"was not run: its output file $out could not be opened for writing"} ;;
                *)               clause=${clause//\{rc\}/"none (the read was not run: its output file $out could not be opened for writing)"} ;;
            esac
            unscanned "${clause//\{detail\}/}"
            return 1
        fi
        case "$errs" in
            quiet) "$@" >&9 2>/dev/null || read_rc=$? ;;
            merge) "$@" >&9 2>&1 || read_rc=$? ;;
            *)     "$@" >&9 || read_rc=$? ;;
        esac
        exec 9>&-
    elif [ "$keep" -ne 0 ]; then
        # A marker byte after the output, stripped below: the substitution drops
        # trailing newlines, and a target of a newline alone would read as empty.
        case "$errs" in
            quiet) read_out=$(set +e; "$@" 2>/dev/null; s=$?; printf x; exit "$s") || read_rc=$? ;;
            merge) read_out=$(set +e; "$@" 2>&1; s=$?; printf x; exit "$s") || read_rc=$? ;;
            *)     read_out=$(set +e; "$@"; s=$?; printf x; exit "$s") || read_rc=$? ;;
        esac
        read_out=${read_out%x}
    else
        case "$errs" in
            quiet) read_out=$("$@" 2>/dev/null) || read_rc=$? ;;
            merge) read_out=$("$@" 2>&1) || read_rc=$? ;;
            *)     read_out=$("$@") || read_rc=$? ;;
        esac
    fi
    case ",$expect," in *",$read_rc,"*) return 0 ;; esac
    # {rc} first, then {detail} by a QUOTED replacement: an unquoted one is
    # rewritten by patsub_replacement (bash 5.2, on by default), which turned
    # every & of the -d function's answer into the matched text and a \& into
    # &, and the answer, spliced first, was re-scanned for {rc}, so a path
    # holding that text printed with the status in its place (the round 6
    # refuters, 2026-09-22; tests/pre-push-hook.bats, the fourth-arm case
    # over such a path).
    clause=${clause//\{rc\}/"$read_rc"}
    [ -z "$detail" ] || clause=${clause//\{detail\}/"$("$detail" || :)"}
    unscanned "$clause"
    return 1
}
# Whether each scan WOULD run: the identifier scan when the denylist file exists
# (ROMP_PRIVATE_STRINGS, else the XDG default), the credential scan when it is
# not skipped (ROMP_NO_GITLEAKS) and a gitleaks binary resolves and is
# executable (ROMP_GITLEAKS, else the first on PATH). Each scan's own early
# return reads its predicate, and so does refuse_replace_refs, so the gate and
# the scans agree on whether a scan is armed; the gate is per clone (its own
# note). The predicates decide only whether a scan runs: an empty denylist arms
# a scan that greps for nothing, and a skipped or absent scanner keeps its
# silence or its notice in scan_credentials. Each is called as a condition (an
# `if`, an `||`), where set -e does not act on it.
private_strings_file() {
    printf '%s' "${ROMP_PRIVATE_STRINGS:-${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/romp/private-strings.txt}"
}
identifier_scan_armed() {
    [ -f "$(private_strings_file)" ]
}
gitleaks_binary() {   # the scanner's path when one resolves and is executable; nothing otherwise, status 0 either way
    local gl
    gl="${ROMP_GITLEAKS:-$(command -v gitleaks || true)}"
    if [ -n "$gl" ] && [ -x "$gl" ]; then printf '%s' "$gl"; fi
}
credential_scan_armed() {
    [ -z "${ROMP_NO_GITLEAKS:-}" ] && [ -n "$(gitleaks_binary)" ]
}

# A clone that carries a replace ref is refused before either scan runs (the
# header's premise): under a replacement, what a scan reads and what the push
# transfers can differ, and the push is refused rather than scanned. Only where
# a scan would otherwise run: the refusal exists because a replace ref makes
# the scanned bytes diverge from the transferred bytes, so a CLEAN REPORT would
# be false; where neither scan runs there is no report and nothing false to
# prevent, and a clone that never asked for either scan is unaffected (the
# header). The gate is per CLONE: armed on the two predicates alone, read once
# ahead of the per-ref loops, and refusing on any replace ref the clone holds,
# whether or not the replaced object is in the push. So a push a scan would
# read nothing of (a deletion of a remote ref alone, which both scans skip
# before reading a byte; a denylist of comments alone, which arms a scan that
# greps for nothing) is refused under a replace ref too. Fail-safe, and left so
# on purpose: the per-ref reads live in two loops, one per scan, and a gate
# inside them would refuse twice and list the refs twice. Each ref is named;
# GIT_REPLACE_REF_BASE moves the namespace git reads, so the same variable says
# where to look. A listing that failed refuses too: whether the reads and the
# push agree is then unknown.
refuse_replace_refs() {
    local found ref
    identifier_scan_armed || credential_scan_armed || return 0
    judged_read own="the REPLACE REFS listing" 0 "the REPLACE REFS of this clone could not be listed (git for-each-ref exited {rc}), so whether the scans read what the push transfers is unknown" -- git for-each-ref --format='%(refname)' "${GIT_REPLACE_REF_BASE:-refs/replace/}" || :
    found=$read_out
    [ -n "$found" ] || return 0
    while IFS= read -r ref; do
        echo "romp pre-push: this clone carries a replace ref ($ref): under it what a scan reads and what the push transfers can differ, so the push is refused rather than scanned" >&2
    done <<< "$found"
    echo "  Delete it (git replace -d <object>) and push again, or push from a clone that carries none." >&2
    failed_scan=1
}

# The scratch directory: for the added-lines capture (scan_identifiers), the
# NUL-delimited listings of the binary-verdict check (refuse_hidden_paths) and
# the credential feed (scan_credentials),
# made once, under TMPDIR, by whichever read comes first, and removed by the
# EXIT trap when the hook ends. Status 1 when mktemp failed, scratch
# left empty, so each caller refuses its own read as unscanned naming mktemp
# and a later caller tries again rather than writing under an empty name.
make_scratch() {
    [ -z "$scratch" ] || return 0
    scratch=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/romp-pre-push.XXXXXX") || { scratch=""; return 1; }
}

# The scratch listings are NUL-delimited (a path may hold any byte but NUL and
# the slash); the joins in refuse_hidden_paths read them line by line, so each
# is first checked to hold no newline byte, then rewritten with a newline for
# each NUL. A path with a newline (legal to git, seen nowhere) fails the check,
# and the caller refuses the push as unscanned rather than judge it by nothing.
# The newline test is read by the shell alone, as nul_records reads the same
# files: each NUL-terminated record, and any bytes after the last NUL, is
# checked for an embedded newline, with no tool between the file and the
# verdict; a file the shell cannot open is refused naming the test, never
# read as a file with no newline. Until round 9a the test was a pipeline
# (tr -cd '\n' piped to wc -c), and a tr silent or cut short with exit 0
# handed wc a whole smaller count, 0, the one passing answer, for a listing
# holding a newline path, so later refusals named the wrong causes (the round
# 8 refuter, 2026-09-24). Each rewrite reads its own status: a tr that failed
# on one file and ran on the next would otherwise hand a join an empty rewrite
# with no status to read (the tip judging nothing; a commit passing unjudged),
# the function's status being its LAST file's alone. A tool that failed is
# status 2, the tool and its status named in the refusal printed for the
# caller's line. Each ANSWER is read as well as each status (the header's
# second class): a tr that exits 0 and writes nothing, or too little, would
# hand a join an empty or truncated rewrite under a clean status, so the
# rewrite's byte count is compared with its input's (wc -c on each; a newline
# for each NUL keeps the count), and every count must be a run of digits
# before any arithmetic reads it, since bash reads an empty operand as zero.
# Each read goes through judged_read with the caller's PREFIX (the line's
# subject: which listings, of the tip or of which commit, could not be
# rewritten), so a tool that failed is refused here naming the read, and a
# count that is not a number, or a rewrite whose count differs from its
# input's, is refused here too, naming the rewrite and both raw answers;
# status 2 tells the caller a refusal was made.
as_lines() {   # <prefix: "the LISTINGS of ... could not be rewritten for the BINARY VERDICT check"> <file>...: rewrites each as <file>.nl; status 1 when one holds a newline, 2 when the file could not be opened for the test, or a rewrite or a count failed or answered short (refused here)
    local prefix="$1" f rec nl a b
    shift
    for f in "$@"; do
        nl=0
        while IFS= read -r -d '' rec || [ -n "$rec" ]; do
            case "$rec" in *$'\n'*) nl=1; break ;; esac
        done < "$f" || { unscanned "$prefix (the newline test could not open ${f##*/})"; return 2; }
        [ "$nl" -eq 0 ] || return 1
        judged_read gate="the REWRITE of a scratch listing" 0 "$prefix (tr exited {rc})" -o "$f.nl" -- tr '\0' '\n' < "$f" || return 2
        judged_read gate="the BYTE COUNT of a scratch listing" 0 "$prefix (the byte count's wc exited {rc} for ${f##*/})" -- wc -c < "$f" || return 2
        a=$read_out
        judged_read gate="the BYTE COUNT of a scratch listing" 0 "$prefix (the byte count's wc exited {rc} for the rewrite of ${f##*/})" -- wc -c < "$f.nl" || return 2
        b=$read_out
        case "${a//[[:space:]]/}" in ''|*[!0-9]*) unscanned "$prefix (the byte count's wc answered \"$a\" for ${f##*/}, not a count)"; return 2 ;; esac
        case "${b//[[:space:]]/}" in ''|*[!0-9]*) unscanned "$prefix (the byte count's wc answered \"$b\" for the rewrite of ${f##*/}, not a count)"; return 2 ;; esac
        [ "${a//[[:space:]]/}" -eq "${b//[[:space:]]/}" ] || { unscanned "$prefix (the rewrite of ${f##*/} wrote ${b//[[:space:]]/} bytes for ${a//[[:space:]]/} read; tr exited 0)"; return 2; }
    done
}
# The count of NUL-terminated records in a scratch file, read by the shell
# alone (no tool between the file and the count), for the gates on the two
# candidate joins in refuse_hidden_paths: the rows a join appended are counted
# this way, before and after the call, and compared with a count in hand (the
# tip) or the count the join's awk recorded (a commit). A last record with no
# NUL is not counted, so a stage cut off mid-record answers short. Sets
# records, the caller's.
nul_records() {   # <file>
    records=0
    while IFS= read -r -d '' _; do records=$((records + 1)); done < "$1"
}

# One numstat, as NUL-delimited rows "<b or t><r or .>\t<path>": b for a
# dash in each count (git's word that it printed no hunk: binary), t
# otherwise; r when the row was a rename (its old and new path follow the
# counts as two more records; the new path is the one kept). The arguments
# after the output file are diff-tree's: -M --root <rev> for a one-parent
# commit against its parent, or two trees and a pathspec. The read goes
# through judged_read with the caller's clause; status 1 when it was refused
# there (the rows read from whatever git printed all the same). Its empty
# answer is the join's to judge: a post-image no row answers for is a short
# read there.
numstat_rows() {   # <clause for a diff-tree that failed, {rc} its status> <out> <diff-tree arguments...>
    local clause=$1 out=$2 rc=0 row name kind
    shift 2
    judged_read gate="the NUMSTAT of a commit" 0 "$clause" -o "$out.raw" -- git diff-tree -r --numstat -z --no-commit-id "$@" || rc=1
    : > "$out"
    while IFS= read -r -d '' row; do
        name=${row#*$'\t'*$'\t'}; kind=.
        if [ -z "$name" ]; then IFS= read -r -d '' _ || true; IFS= read -r -d '' name || name=""; kind=r; fi
        case "$row" in -$'\t'-$'\t'*) printf 'b%s\t%s\0' "$kind" "$name" ;; *) printf 't%s\t%s\0' "$kind" "$name" ;; esac >> "$out"
    done < "$out.raw"
    return "$rc"
}

# A merge's combined patch (the file given: diff-tree -p -c, the read the scan
# makes for a merge, captured by the caller with its status), as NUL-delimited
# rows "<b, t or e>.\t<path>", one per section: a `diff --combined` header and
# what follows it to the next header. b for a "Binary files differ" line
# (git's word that it printed no hunk), t for a hunk (an @@ line), e for a
# header alone (an added empty file, which has no hunk; a mode change over
# content some parent had at the path, set aside as unchanged before the
# join, or under another, a rename candidate there). A line inside a hunk
# begins with one column per parent (a space, a plus or a minus), so a
# header, a Binary line or a hunk line is
# never mistaken for content, and the Binary line is read in the header
# alone, as added_lines reads the +++ line. The header's path is unquoted as
# git quotes it: a path with a byte git escapes (a quote, a backslash, a
# control character; any byte over 127 unless core.quotePath is false) is
# printed in double quotes with C escapes, and a plain one bare, so a plain
# path is taken whole (a leading or trailing space included) and a quoted one
# has its escapes undone byte by byte. Any other `diff --` header ends a
# section and owns none, so a path under one meets no row and is refused as
# unscanned by the join.
combined_rows() {   # <patch file>
    LC_ALL=C awk '
        BEGIN { esc["a"] = 7; esc["b"] = 8; esc["t"] = 9; esc["n"] = 10; esc["v"] = 11; esc["f"] = 12; esc["r"] = 13 }
        function unquote(s,    out, n, i, c, d, v, k) {
            if (substr(s, 1, 1) != "\"") return s
            out = ""; n = length(s); i = 2
            while (i <= n) {
                c = substr(s, i, 1)
                if (c == "\"") break
                if (c != "\\") { out = out c; i++; continue }
                i++; d = substr(s, i, 1)
                if (d ~ /^[0-7]$/) {
                    v = 0; k = 0
                    while (k < 3 && substr(s, i, 1) ~ /^[0-7]$/) { v = v * 8 + substr(s, i, 1); i++; k++ }
                    out = out sprintf("%c", v); continue
                }
                out = out ((d in esc) ? sprintf("%c", esc[d]) : d)
                i++
            }
            return out
        }
        function flush() { if (path != "") printf "%s.\t%s%c", kind, path, 0; path = "" }
        /^diff --combined / { flush(); path = unquote(substr($0, 17)); kind = "e"; hdr = 1; next }
        /^diff --cc /       { flush(); path = unquote(substr($0, 11)); kind = "e"; hdr = 1; next }
        /^diff --/          { flush(); hdr = 0; next }
        hdr && /^Binary files / { kind = "b"; next }
        /^@@/               { if (hdr && path != "") kind = "t"; hdr = 0; next }
        END { flush() }' "$1"
}

# The joins and pipelines refuse_hidden_paths passes to judged_read whole (each
# one's status is its awk's, or its tr's, through pipefail), their programs as
# the check has read them since round 3. The two that print the CANDIDATES
# (tip_candidates, join_candidates) are gated by the caller on a count (the
# header's second class): the tip's against a count in hand, the -z listing's
# regular files with bytes less the grep's read list; a commit's against the
# count its awk records of the rows it printed (round 7: an awk or a tr
# exiting 0 and writing nothing emptied either, and a hidden text file was
# published through a real push, the round 6 refuters, 2026-09-22).
tip_unlisted_read_file() {   # <tip sha> <listing.nl> <read.nl>: the first file the grep lists as read that the -z listing lacks
    LC_ALL=C awk -v sha="$1" '
        FILENAME == ARGV[1] { i = index($0, "\t"); listed[substr($0, i + 1)] = 1; next }
        { if (substr($0, 1, length(sha) + 1) == sha ":") { p = substr($0, length(sha) + 2); if (!(p in listed)) { print p; exit } } }' "$2" "$3"
}
tip_blobs() {   # <listing.nl>: the blob shas of the tree's regular files and symlinks, one per line
    LC_ALL=C awk '{ i = index($0, "\t"); split(substr($0, 1, i - 1), f, " "); if (f[1] == "100644" || f[1] == "100755" || f[1] == "120000") print f[3] }' "$1"
}
tip_candidates() {   # <tip sha> <read.nl> <listing.nl>: "tip\t<blob>\t<path>" per NUL for each regular file with bytes that the grep did not list
    LC_ALL=C awk -v sha="$1" '
        FILENAME == ARGV[1] { if (substr($0, 1, length(sha) + 1) == sha ":") read[substr($0, length(sha) + 2)] = 1; next }
        { i = index($0, "\t"); split(substr($0, 1, i - 1), f, " "); path = substr($0, i + 1)
          if ((f[1] == "100644" || f[1] == "100755") && f[4] + 0 > 0 && !(path in read)) printf "tip\t%s\t%s\n", f[3], path }' "$2" "$3" | tr '\n' '\0'
}
merge_rename_candidates() {   # <gone.nl> <post.nl>: the post-image paths whose blob some parent held under a path the merge lacks, one per NUL
    LC_ALL=C awk 'FILENAME == ARGV[1] { gone[$0] = 1; next } { i = index($0, "\t"); if (substr($0, 1, i - 1) in gone) print substr($0, i + 1) }' "$1" "$2" | tr '\n' '\0'
}
unlisted_verdict_path() {   # <listed.nl> <rows.nl> <rows2.nl> <rows3.nl>: the first path a verdict names that the listing did not
    LC_ALL=C awk 'FILENAME == ARGV[1] { listed[$0] = 1; next } { p = substr($0, 4); if (!(p in listed)) { print p; exit } }' "$@"
}
byte_counts() {   # <blob-ish>: "<bytes> <NULs>" over the first 8000 bytes, counted with their NULs; the rest is drained so the cat-file is never cut off mid-write (the group's status is the drain's, so the status is the cat-file's)
    git cat-file blob "$1" | { head -c 8000 | LC_ALL=C od -An -v -tu1 | awk '{ for (i = 1; i <= NF; i++) { b++; if ($i == 0) z++ } } END { print b + 0, z + 0 }'; cat > /dev/null; }
}

join_candidates() {   # <rev> <merge> <short file> <count file> <rows.nl> <rows2.nl> <gone.nl> <links.nl> <tpaths.nl> <rows3.nl> <tipblobs> <post.nl>: the candidate entries, one per NUL, their count written to the count file at the end; status 3 or 4 for a short read (refuse_hidden_paths says which)
    local rev=$1 merge=$2 short_file=$3 count_file=$4
    shift 4
    # The short file's path and the count file's reach awk through the
    # ENVIRONMENT, never a -v operand: awk escape-processes a -v value, so
    # under a TMPDIR carrying a backslash pair gawk warned on every clean push
    # (a backslash before a letter it has no escape for) and ended the join on
    # a failed redirect at a short read (a backslash-n, the path cut at a
    # newline), the path unnamed (the round 5 refuters, 2026-09-22). The two
    # -v flags left carry a sha and a digit, never a path.
    ROMP_SHORT_FILE=$short_file ROMP_COUNT_FILE=$count_file LC_ALL=C awk -v rev="$rev" -v merge="$merge" '
        BEGIN { short_file = ENVIRON["ROMP_SHORT_FILE"]; count_file = ENVIRON["ROMP_COUNT_FILE"] }
        FILENAME == ARGV[1] { p = substr($0, 4); seenm[p] = 1; if (substr($0, 1, 1) == "b") binm[p] = 1; if (substr($0, 1, 1) == "t") hunk[p] = 1; if (substr($0, 2, 1) == "r") ren[p] = 1; next }
        FILENAME == ARGV[2] { p = substr($0, 4); seenn[p] = 1; if (substr($0, 1, 1) == "b") binn[p] = 1; next }
        FILENAME == ARGV[3] { gone[$0] = 1; next }
        FILENAME == ARGV[4] { link[$0] = 1; next }
        FILENAME == ARGV[5] { typed[$0] = 1; next }
        FILENAME == ARGV[6] { p = substr($0, 4); seent[p] = 1; if (substr($0, 1, 1) == "b") bint[p] = 1; next }
        FILENAME == ARGV[7] { tip[$0] = 1; next }
        { i = index($0, "\t"); blob = substr($0, 1, i - 1); path = substr($0, i + 1)
          if (blob in tip) next
          if (merge) {
              if (path in hunk) next
              if (!(path in binm)) {
                  if (path in typed) {
                      if (!(path in seent)) { if (!short) { short = 4; print path > short_file }; next }
                      if (!(path in bint)) next }
                  else if (!(path in seenm)) { if (!short) { short = 3; print path > short_file }; next } } }
          else {
              if (!(path in seenm) || !(path in seenn) || ((path in typed) && !(path in seent))) { if (!short) { short = 3; print path > short_file }; next }
              if (path in typed) { if (!(path in bint)) next }
              else {
                  if (!(path in binm)) next
                  if ((path in ren) && (blob in gone) && !(path in binn)) next } }
          printf "%s%s%s%s\t%s\n", rev, ((path in ren) ? "+rename" : ""), ((path in link) ? "+link" : ""), (((path in typed) && !(merge && (path in binm))) ? "+typed" : ""), $0; printed++ }
        END { print printed + 0 > count_file; if (short) exit short }' "$@" | tr '\n' '\0'
}

# The fourth arm's rider for the join (judged_read -d, composed after the
# read): whether the join had written the short file before its pipeline
# failed. Under pipefail a tr that failed after the awk exited 3 or 4 hides
# that status and leaves the path recorded, so the path is stated as a fact
# the join recorded, never as a short read the status does not establish.
join_short_detail() {
    local path=""
    { IFS= read -r path || :; } < "$scratch/short"
    if [ -n "$path" ]; then
        printf '%s' "; the join had recorded $path as a post-image met no verdict for before the pipeline failed"
    else
        printf '%s' "; the join had recorded no post-image as met no verdict for"
    fi
}

# The blobs the identifier scan's two content reads SKIPPED as binary while
# their bytes are text (the header): judged by git's own verdict from each
# read, never by a rule of the hook's. The tip: the tree's regular files
# (ls-tree -r -z -l; a symlink's target is read whole by the symlink pass, by
# cat-file, whatever any attribute or key says, checked by execution
# 2026-09-21, and a gitlink is no blob) less the non-empty files the same git
# grep -I lists as READ (-l with an empty pattern, under the binary rule the
# content grep applies), the empty ones set aside by their size. Each new
# commit: the post-image BLOBS of its changed-path listing (--raw --no-renames
# -c, so a rename is a deletion and an addition, and a merge lists the paths
# that differ from every parent; every path it names, any status and mode, is
# kept as the population its verdicts below must agree with, the header's
# second class: a path a numstat or the combined patch answers for that the
# listing did not name is a listing that answered short, refused as unscanned
# naming the listing and that path, so an emptied listing cannot pass a
# commit as one that changed nothing): a regular file or a symlink (modes 100644,
# 100755 and 120000; a gitlink, 160000, names a commit, and a tree is no
# post-image), less a MODE-ONLY change, whose post-image blob is every
# pre-image blob (the diff prints its mode lines and no hunk and no Binary
# line, so it hid nothing new, while the pair's stat calls an unchanged blob
# over the size key binary: a mode-only change of such a file was refused for
# it, the round 3 auditor 2026-09-21), and less a blob the TIP's tree holds
# under any path (the same ls-tree, its blob shas kept), which is the tip
# half's to judge: its grep read the blob or skipped it and refused it above,
# or its symlink pass read the target, so the per-commit half judges only
# content absent from the tip (a clean big text file added at the tip under
# the size key was refused on the commit line while the tip's grep had read
# it, the round 3 auditors 2026-09-21); when the tip's own reads failed, the
# push is refused as unscanned above and the per-commit half judges every
# candidate, the safe side of a refusal already made. For a commit with ONE
# parent, those that git diff-tree --numstat reports as binary (a dash for
# each count) over the pair the per-commit diff reads (-M, so a renamed file
# is judged with its old path as the diff judges it): the diff's Binary line
# and the numstat's dashes agree on every blob with bytes, so the decision
# is the diff's own, and an empty blob passes either way (an empty blob
# under such a rule is the one pair the numstat calls binary while the diff
# prints its header alone; it is a candidate here and passes as empty at the
# byte stage, the outcome the diff's own). A pure rename (the new path's
# blob among the commit's deletions) is the one pair the diff prints nothing
# for, and is
# judged as the addition of its new path by a second numstat (--no-renames):
# hidden when the new path hides the blob, the rule the rename refusal set,
# and not when an explicit diff line for the new path keeps it text. A TYPE
# change (status T in a one-parent commit's listing: a file replaced by a
# symlink, or the reverse) is the one pair the diff prints as two, a deletion
# and an addition each judged alone, while the pair's stat is binary when
# either side is: it is judged as the addition of its new object by a third
# numstat, the empty tree against the commit's tree for those paths (each a
# :(literal) pathspec, a name and never a pattern), the read the diff makes
# for the addition; judged by the pair, a binary file replaced by a symlink
# was refused as hidden text the diff had printed in full (found by execution
# 2026-09-21). For a MERGE, the combined patch itself (diff-tree -p -c, the
# read the scan makes for one), read again and parsed section by section
# (combined_rows): its verdict follows no pairwise read, since it applies no
# size rule and applies a path's diff attribute to a symlink, which the
# pairwise diff does not. A section printing "Binary files differ" is a path
# the patch printed no hunk for, a candidate; a section with a hunk was read;
# a path with no hunk and no Binary line (no section at all, or the header
# alone) whose blob some parent holds under a path the merge lacks is a pure
# rename, the one change the patch prints nothing for, judged as the
# addition of its new path by the third numstat, as a one-parent commit's
# is (the candidates are read from the merge's deletions against EACH
# parent, diff-tree --raw -m, since the combined listing names a deletion
# only when every parent held the path, and content two parents held under
# two paths, moved to a third with both gone, is a deletion against each
# parent alone: it met no candidate and was refused as unscanned, the round
# 3 auditor 2026-09-22); such a path that is no
# rename is a candidate when its section is the header alone (an added empty
# file prints its header and no hunk, and the byte stage passes an empty
# blob; a non-empty blob so printed is text the patch printed no hunk for,
# refused as hidden), and a verdict the read owed and did not print when it
# has no section at all, refused as unscanned.
# Judged against each parent, a merge's own symlink under -diff, the string
# in its target, gone at the tip, was published by a real push, and a big
# text file resolved in a merge was refused as hidden while the patch had
# printed its hunk (the round 3 auditors, 2026-09-21). A post-image the
# listing names and a numstat does not answer for refuses as unscanned:
# a short answer is not an answer of "text". The listings are read with -z
# into a scratch directory, so every path meets its verdict byte for byte, and
# joined line by line under as_lines' guard. A blob so named is then read
# (cat-file) and judged by git's own byte rule with no diff machinery: a NUL
# in the first 8000 bytes is binary, and passes as binaries always have;
# anything else is text the scan could not see, named with the tip or the
# commit, and the push is refused rather than scanned. An empty blob hides
# nothing and passes. The line quotes the path's diff attribute, one
# check-attr per refused path and for the report alone: `unset` (a -diff line,
# the binary macro) and a driver whose diff.<driver>.binary reads true are
# named as before, the driver first (a driver may be named `unset` too); any
# other answer (unspecified, set, a driver with no such setting; the two
# reserved words are also names a driver may take, so no answer decides
# anything here) names no attribute the hook can point at, and the line states
# the blob's size and the clone's core.bigFileThreshold instead, facts and not
# a cause, unless the file is a changed file whose previous version is binary
# by its bytes (the pair is binary when either side is, whatever the key
# says, so the diff printed no text for the new version; a merge's combined
# patch prints Binary the same way when a parent's version is binary): that
# line names the previous version as the cause, with the two facts beside it
# where the key is set, and the advice names the fetch remedy (the header)
# and not the key's, which lifts nothing there. The previous version is read
# for the report alone, as the attribute is, key or no key: for a one-parent
# commit in its parent, at the path the diff read the change from when it
# read it as a rename (the old path re-derived at report time from the
# commit's raw listing with -M, an R row's two path records, never carried as
# a field of the rows, since a path may hold a tab); for a merge in each
# parent in turn, the parent count the caller read (rev-list --parents), the
# first parent whose version holds a NUL named; never for a type change, nor
# for a merge's rename candidate the patch printed no section for, each
# judged by the addition of the new object alone, where the previous version
# is no cause (a rename candidate whose section printed Binary was judged by
# the patch, so each parent's version is read for it: marked as judged by
# the addition, a text file moved onto a path a parent holds a binary file
# at took the key's facts and the key's advice with no cause named; the
# round 5 refuters, 2026-09-22). A read that fails, or a path
# with no previous version (an addition, a root commit), leaves the line
# with its two facts. A one-parent commit's symlink reads no attribute (the
# pairwise diff applies none to a symlink) and its line says so, with the
# same two facts; a
# merge's symlink reads its path's attribute as a file does, since the
# combined patch applied it. A read that fails (a listing, the grep, the
# numstats, the combined patch, the empty tree's name, a cat-file, the
# scratch directory, a listing's rewrite for the joins) refuses as
# unscanned, naming the read; whatever was
# listed is still judged; a check-attr that fails or answers about another
# path leaves the refusal standing, the failure in the attribute's place. A
# read that exits 0 and answers SHORT refuses as unscanned too, where a
# second read in hand shows it short (the header's second class), naming the
# read and both answers: the tip's -z listing against the entry count of the
# symlink pass's listing of the same tree (a different number, long or
# short) and against the grep's read list (a file read must be one listed);
# a commit's changed-path listing against the paths its numstats or its
# combined patch name; a listing's rewrite against its input's byte count
# (as_lines); the byte judge's count of zero against the blob's size
# (cat-file -s: an empty answer is empty content only when the blob is). Each
# is gated on the read's own status 0 and, for the listing, on its verdicts
# having exited 0, so a read that failed is refused once, on its status, and
# not again with a wrong cause; whatever was listed is still judged. The
# tip is not listed again when its first listing (the symlink pass) failed or
# found no tree: the failure is reported there once, and a pushed object that
# peels to a blob has no path an attribute could name.
refuse_hidden_paths() {   # <local_ref> <local_sha> <revs: one "<sha> <parent count>" per line> <tip listed: 1 or 0> <the symlink pass's entry count>
    local local_ref="$1" local_sha="$2" revs="$3" tip_listed="$4" tip_entries="$5"
    local rc rev n entry meta path mode blob place fields k i how says size threshold apath attr value counts bytes nuls numstat_ok renamed
    local status unchanged islink merge prior facts empty_tree="" listing_rc count typed prev where rsrc rdst at accounts unread
    local records before appended joined files listed_read counted tip_agreed
    local -a tpaths
    make_scratch || { unscanned "the BINARY VERDICTS on the paths $local_ref (${local_sha:0:10}) publishes could not be checked (no scratch directory: mktemp failed)"; return 0; }
    : > "$scratch/entries"      # "<tip, or the commit's sha>\t<blob>\t<path>", one per NUL: the blobs git called binary
    : > "$scratch/tipblobs"     # the blob shas of the tip's tree, one per line: a per-commit candidate among them is the tip half's to judge
    if [ "$tip_listed" -ne 0 ]; then
        # The -z listing (its empty answer gated below against the symlink pass's
        # entry count and the grep's read list) and the read list, by the same
        # grep: exit 1 is a tree it read nothing from (empty, or of empty files),
        # not a failure, so 0 and 1 are its expected set; its empty answer sends
        # every regular file with bytes to the byte judge below, the safe side,
        # so the join is its gate.
        listing_rc=0
        judged_read gate="the -z listing of the tip" 0 "the TREE of the tip of $local_ref (${local_sha:0:10}) could not be listed for the BINARY VERDICT check (git ls-tree exited {rc})" -o "$scratch/listing" -- git ls-tree -r -z -l "$local_sha" || listing_rc=$read_rc
        if judged_read gate="the READ LIST of the grep of the tip" 0,1 "the FILES the grep of the tip of $local_ref (${local_sha:0:10}) reads could not be listed for the BINARY VERDICT check (git grep exited {rc})" -q -o "$scratch/read" -- git grep --no-color -I -l -z -e '' "$local_sha" --; then
            # The two listings rewritten for the joins: as_lines returns 1 for a
            # path holding a newline and 2 for a listing the newline test could
            # not open, or a rewrite or a count that failed or answered short
            # (refused there, naming the read); either
            # is refused as unscanned rather than judged by an empty rewrite.
            rc=0; as_lines "the LISTINGS of the tip of $local_ref (${local_sha:0:10}) could not be rewritten for the BINARY VERDICT check" "$scratch/listing" "$scratch/read" || rc=$?
            if [ "$rc" -eq 1 ]; then
                unscanned "a path at the tip of $local_ref (${local_sha:0:10}) holds a newline, which the BINARY VERDICT check cannot judge"
            elif [ "$rc" -eq 0 ]; then
                # The -z listing against the two reads in hand (the header's second
                # class), when it exited 0: its record count against the entry count
                # of the symlink pass's plain listing of the same tree (a different
                # number either way: a listing can answer long as well as short), and
                # every file the grep lists as read against the paths it names. An
                # emptied or shortened listing would otherwise empty the tip's
                # complement and its blob set, and a hidden text file at the tip, the
                # remote already holding it, would be judged by nothing. Each count
                # is a run of digits before -eq reads it. The record count bounds
                # the listing's END only when it counts WHOLE entries, so the grep
                # counts a line only through the tab that ends its size column (a
                # mode, a type, an object name and a size ahead of it): a record
                # cut inside counts as none, where grep -c . counted it, and a
                # hidden text file whose record was the last one, cut before its
                # tab, met neither the file count nor the join and published
                # through a real push (round 8b3, 2026-09-23). A record cut after
                # its tab keeps its object name whole and is judged by it.
                tip_agreed=1            # cleared below when the listing lacks a file the grep read, or that comparison was refused: the candidate count is judged only on agreement, so a listing refused there is not refused again with a second cause
                if [ "$listing_rc" -eq 0 ]; then
                    # grep -c exits 1 for a count of zero and prints it, so 0 and 1
                    # are its expected set; the count is a run of digits below. The
                    # set is the whole gate against a grep -c that prints the count
                    # and exits 2 (the count then agrees with the symlink pass, so
                    # the digit check and the comparison refuse nothing):
                    # tests/pre-push-hook.bats pins that shape through a real push.
                    if judged_read gate="the ENTRY COUNT of the -z listing" 0,1 "the TREE of the tip of $local_ref (${local_sha:0:10}) could not be counted for the BINARY VERDICT check (grep -c exited {rc})" -- grep -c "^[0-7]\{6\} [a-z][a-z]* [0-9a-f][0-9a-f]*  *[-0-9][0-9]*"$'\t' "$scratch/listing.nl"; then
                        count=$read_out
                        if [ -z "$count" ] || [ -n "${count//[0-9]/}" ] || [ -z "$tip_entries" ] || [ -n "${tip_entries//[0-9]/}" ]; then
                            unscanned "the TREE of the tip of $local_ref (${local_sha:0:10}) could not be counted for the BINARY VERDICT check (the -z listing's entries counted as \"$count\" and the symlink pass's as \"$tip_entries\", not two counts)"
                        elif [ "$count" -ne "$tip_entries" ]; then
                            unscanned "the TREE of the tip of $local_ref (${local_sha:0:10}) was listed short or long for the BINARY VERDICT check (git ls-tree -r -z -l exited 0 and listed $count entries where the symlink pass's git ls-tree -r of the same tree listed $tip_entries)"
                        fi
                    fi
                    if judged_read own="the TIP LISTING join against the read list" 0 "the TREE of the tip of $local_ref (${local_sha:0:10}) could not be compared with the grep's read list for the BINARY VERDICT check (awk exited {rc})" -- tip_unlisted_read_file "$local_sha" "$scratch/listing.nl" "$scratch/read.nl"; then
                        path=$read_out
                        if [ -n "$path" ]; then
                            tip_agreed=0
                            unscanned "the TREE of the tip of $local_ref (${local_sha:0:10}) was listed short for the BINARY VERDICT check (git ls-tree -r -z -l exited 0 and its listing lacks $path, a file the grep of the tip lists as read)"
                        fi
                    else
                        tip_agreed=0
                    fi
                fi
                # The tip's blobs, for the per-commit half: a candidate blob the tip
                # holds under any path was judged here, by the grep (read, or skipped
                # and refused below) or by the symlink pass, and is not judged again.
                # An emptied answer sends every per-commit candidate to the byte
                # judge, the safe side.
                judged_read own="the TIP BLOBS of the -z listing" 0 "the TREE of the tip of $local_ref (${local_sha:0:10}) could not be read for its blobs for the BINARY VERDICT check (awk exited {rc})" -o "$scratch/tipblobs" -- tip_blobs "$scratch/listing.nl" || :
                # "<mode> blob <sha> <size>\t<path>" against "<tree-ish>:<path>": a
                # regular file with bytes that the grep did not list is one it skipped.
                # The join's rows are judged against a count already in hand (the
                # header's second class): the -z listing's regular files with bytes
                # (the join's own predicate, mode 100644 or 100755 and a size over
                # zero, read by grep -c in ls-tree's padded size column) less the
                # files the grep of the tip lists as read (each a regular file with
                # bytes, so the difference is the candidate count) must equal the
                # rows appended, counted by their NUL terminators in the shell
                # (nul_records); another number refuses the push naming the read and
                # the three counts, where an awk or a tr exiting 0 and writing
                # nothing emptied the candidates and a hidden text file the remote
                # already holds was published through a real push (the round 6
                # refuters, 2026-09-22). Gated on the listing having exited 0 and
                # agreed with the read list above, so a listing refused there is
                # not refused again with a second cause; each count is a run of
                # digits before the arithmetic reads it.
                nul_records "$scratch/entries"; before=$records
                if judged_read gate="the TIP CANDIDATES join" 0 "the TREE of the tip of $local_ref (${local_sha:0:10}) could not be joined with the grep's read list for the BINARY VERDICT check (awk or tr exited {rc})" -a "$scratch/entries" -- tip_candidates "$local_sha" "$scratch/read.nl" "$scratch/listing.nl" \
                    && [ "$listing_rc" -eq 0 ] && [ "$tip_agreed" -ne 0 ]; then
                    nul_records "$scratch/entries"; appended=$((records - before))
                    counted=1; files=""; listed_read=""
                    if judged_read gate="the FILE COUNT of the -z listing" 0,1 "the TREE of the tip of $local_ref (${local_sha:0:10}) could not be counted for the BINARY VERDICT check (grep -c over the -z listing exited {rc})" -- grep -c -E $'^(100644|100755) blob [0-9a-f]+ +[1-9][0-9]*\t' "$scratch/listing.nl"; then files=$read_out; else counted=0; fi
                    if judged_read gate="the LINE COUNT of the read list" 0,1 "the TREE of the tip of $local_ref (${local_sha:0:10}) could not be counted for the BINARY VERDICT check (grep -c over the grep's read list exited {rc})" -- grep -c . "$scratch/read.nl"; then listed_read=$read_out; else counted=0; fi
                    if [ "$counted" -ne 0 ]; then
                        if [ -z "$files" ] || [ -n "${files//[0-9]/}" ] || [ -z "$listed_read" ] || [ -n "${listed_read//[0-9]/}" ]; then
                            unscanned "the TREE of the tip of $local_ref (${local_sha:0:10}) could not be counted for the BINARY VERDICT check (grep -c counted the -z listing's regular files with bytes as \"$files\" and the grep's read list as \"$listed_read\", not two counts)"
                        elif [ "$appended" -ne "$((files - listed_read))" ]; then
                            unscanned "the TREE of the tip of $local_ref (${local_sha:0:10}) was joined short with the grep's read list for the BINARY VERDICT check (the join appended $appended candidate rows where the -z listing names $files regular files with bytes and the grep's read list $listed_read of them, so the count expected is $((files - listed_read)); awk and tr exited 0)"
                        fi
                    fi
                fi
            fi
        fi
    fi
    : > "$scratch/pcount"       # "<sha> <parent count>", one per line: the count the report below reads a commit's previous versions by
    while read -r -u 3 rev n; do
        [ -n "$rev" ] || continue
        # The listing's empty answer is gated below against its verdicts: a path
        # a numstat row or a combined-patch section names that it did not is a
        # listing that answered short.
        listing_rc=0
        judged_read gate="the CHANGED PATHS listing of a commit" 0 "the CHANGED PATHS of commit ${rev:0:10} could not be listed for the BINARY VERDICT check (git diff-tree exited {rc})" -o "$scratch/listing" -- git diff-tree -r --raw --no-renames --root -c -z --no-commit-id "$rev" || listing_rc=$read_rc
        # Two records per listing entry: ":<modes> <shas> <status>" (a merge's
        # combined line has one more mode, sha and leading colon per parent; the
        # post-image mode and sha come last of their kind) and then the path.
        : > "$scratch/listed"    # every path the listing names, any status and mode, one per NUL: the population the verdicts below must agree with
        : > "$scratch/post"      # "<blob>\t<path>", one per NUL: the post-image blobs (a regular file or a symlink), a mode-only change left out
        : > "$scratch/gone"      # the pre-image blobs of the deletions, one per NUL: a pure rename's new path has its blob here
        : > "$scratch/links"     # the paths of the symlinks among a one-parent commit's post-images, one per NUL: the line for one names no attribute
        : > "$scratch/tpaths"    # the paths judged as the addition of their new object, one per NUL: a one-parent commit's type changes (status T) here; a merge's rename candidates below
        : > "$scratch/short"     # the first post-image the join below met no verdict for, when a read answered short: made here, so the report's read of it is never of a missing file (which set -e would end the hook on)
        # More than one parent: the combined patch's own verdict, not the pair's.
        # Decided by the parent count the caller read (rev-list --parents, once
        # per commit for the added-lines pass, its answer judged there against
        # the commit named first), not by the listing's shape: a listing that
        # answered nothing for a merge would read as a one-parent commit's,
        # whose pair numstat prints nothing for a merge, and the two empty
        # answers would agree. The count is EMPTY here only when its read failed
        # or answered short, each refused there; the listing's shape (one mode
        # and one sha per parent in a record) then decides, on a push already
        # refused.
        merge=0; k=1
        case "$n" in ''|*[!0-9]*) n="" ;; *) [ "$n" -le 1 ] || merge=1 ;; esac
        while IFS= read -r -d '' meta && IFS= read -r -d '' path; do
            printf '%s\0' "$path" >> "$scratch/listed"
            k=${meta%%[^:]*}; k=${#k}
            [ -n "$n" ] || [ "$k" -le 1 ] || merge=1
            # shellcheck disable=SC2206  # the record is deliberately word-split
            fields=($meta)
